// jhonstart/client — the server/client boundary (front 29 of 1.0.10-beta).
//
// Next.js writes `'use client'` at the top of a file and the bundler reads it.
// botopink has no directive syntax and the milestone forbids compiler changes,
// so the boundary is built out of what annotation processing already gives: a
// `@Decl`-first comptime function and `@emit`. `#[client]` marks a component,
// `#[clientProps]` marks the record its props travel in, and the server render
// contributes an ISLAND — `<div data-onze-i="i0">` — whose component name and
// encoded props live in front 23's payload rather than on the element.
//
// ── What is ENFORCED here, and what is NOT ──────────────────────────────────
//
// Read this before reading anything else in this file. The front's priority
// line is a security claim — "without a *checked* boundary, a secret read on
// the server reaches the browser and nobody notices" — and **this file is not
// that check.** Front 29 defines the boundary; **front 68 enforces it at build
// time.** 29 without 68 is a convention nobody walks.
//
// Enforced TODAY, at comptime, as a REFUSAL with no flag that turns it off
// (decision 67 — a boundary that warns is a boundary that is ignored):
//
//   1. `#[client]` on anything but a `fn` — refused, located at the annotation.
//   2. `#[client]` on a fn whose return type is not `Element` — refused. A
//      server component is `#[@future] fn … -> @Future<Element>`, whose
//      reflected `returnType` is `"Future"`, so it cannot be marked client.
//   3. `#[clientProps]` on anything but a record — refused (an enum-shaped
//      `type` has `variants`, a `fn` is not `DeclKind.Type`).
//   4. A field of a `#[clientProps]` record whose type is not one of the four
//      serializable scalars — refused, naming the field and its type.
//
// NOT enforced today, by anything, anywhere in this tree:
//
//   - that a `#[client]` component's parameter record carries `#[clientProps]`
//     at all. `@Decl` exposes `fields`, `variants`, `methods`, `returnType` and
//     `annotations` and **does not expose a function's parameters** — so
//     `#[client]` alone can check nothing about props, which is the entire
//     reason the marker is split in two. A client component declared with a
//     bare `Element` parameter is accepted here.
//   - that no module reachable from a `#[client]` component imports
//     `serverOnly` (the poison pill below), or `request`/`cookies`/`headers`
//     from `server.bp`. Those are module-GRAPH predicates and there is no graph
//     here.
//   - that the props actually written into the payload's `i` row are the ones
//     the `#[clientProps]` record declares. `islandEntry` encodes whatever it
//     is handed.
//
// So today a secret read on the server still reaches the browser if someone
// writes it into an `Island`'s props, and nothing in this file notices. What
// closes it is front 68's walk over the client module graph, and this file's
// § *The front-68 contract* (mirrored in `../../../docs.md`) is the input it
// walks. Nothing here is written as if the decorator were a sandbox.
//
// ── What is NOT declared, and why it is not stubbed ─────────────────────────
//
// The front's § Step 4 asks for three host cells —
// `#[@External.Node("jhonstart/client-runtime", "hydrate")] hydrate()`,
// `#[@External.Node("jhonstart/client-runtime", "islandProps")]
// __onzeClientPropsRaw(name)` and the `propsFor(name)` wrapper over the second.
// **None of them is declared here**, for the two reasons front 27's `link.bp`
// records for its own four cells, re-measured for this file against compiler
// `2e6bb4ac` on 2026-09-21:
//
//   1. `propsFor` would CALL a node-only cell, and a called node-only cell reds
//      the ERLANG build at the call site:
//
//          error: `__cellStatus` has no `#[@External.<Target>(…)]` for the
//                 erlang backend
//           --> src/main.bp:5:12
//
//      `modules/jhonstart` is compiled on BOTH rows, so the wrapper would take
//      every landed assertion in the member off erlang. It is the exact mirror
//      of the erlang-only → commonJS measurement `router.bp` and `server.bp`
//      both carry.
//   2. A DECLARED and never-called node-only cell is fine — that is why
//      `client_runtime.bp`'s `clientRender` does not red erlang, and it was
//      re-measured. So `hydrate()` alone would be cheap. What is not cheap is
//      the module it names: `"jhonstart/client-runtime"` is the bundle **front
//      68** generates and it does not exist in this tree, so the declaration
//      would emit a `require` of a file nobody writes — silently at build time,
//      loudly at run time. A host cell answering what nobody can check is
//      exactly what `router.d.bp`'s one-line `Link` was.
//
// What the browser half needs from this file when front 68's module lands is
// here and is pure: `propsOf(raw)` is the whole decode `propsFor` performs, so
// `propsFor` is then two lines — the cell, and
// `return propsOf(__onzeClientPropsRaw(name));` — and nothing else moves.
// `hydrate()` is per-island: it walks every `[data-onze-i]`, decodes that
// island's props from the payload's `i` row and starts the component. It is
// NOT the bundle entry and it mounts no links and no forms; front 68 generates
// the entry that calls it and then front 27's link mount and front 67's form
// mount once each.
//
// ── The marker is a PURE function, deliberately ─────────────────────────────
//
// `#[client]` emits `pub fn __jhClient_<Name>() -> string`, a pure function
// returning the component's name — not a call into a runtime registry. An
// `@emit` fires on EVERY target, so emitting a call into a Node-only cell would
// make every `#[client]` component fail to link during the erlang server render
// — the exact case the boundary exists to support. A pure marker links
// everywhere and carries the same information: front 68 reads the set of
// `__jhClient_*` names off the module graph.

import {Element} from "element";
import {encodePairs, decodePairs} from "router";

// ── step 1: `#[client]` — the marker ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The `@emit` comes BEFORE the `fail` checks, matching rakun's ordering
// (`decorators.bp`): a failed outcome discards the contributions, so only a
// correctly placed declaration wires up.
//
// Two hard constraints on any decorator body, both of them the reason this one
// is shaped the way it is: it cannot call a sibling function (each decorator is
// lowered ALONE into the eval script), and a `//` comment inside it breaks the
// emitted line. So every predicate below is inlined and the body is bare.
//
// `decl.returnType` is the NAME of the return type, not its spelling: measured
// against `2e6bb4ac`, a `fn … -> Element` reflects as `"Element"` and a
// `#[@future] fn … -> @Future<Element>` reflects as `"Future"`. That is what
// makes check 2 reject a server component — and it is also why the message
// says "returning Element" rather than quoting what was found.
//
// `botopink check` skips decorator invocation entirely, so `__jhClient_<Name>`
// reads as an UNBOUND NAME under `check`. The gate is `botopink test`, never
// `check`. It is the front's third § Language gaps row.
pub fn client(comptime decl: @Decl) {
    @emit("pub fn __jhClient_" + decl.name + "() -> string { return \"" + decl.name + "\"; }");
    if (decl.kind != DeclKind.Fn) decl.fail("#[client] must annotate a function");
    if (decl.returnType != "Element" && decl.returnType != "Component") decl.fail("#[client] must annotate a component returning Element or @Component<Element>");
}

// ── step 2: `#[clientProps]` — the serializable whitelist ───────────────────
//
// The serializability rule is checked where the type information actually is.
// A client component takes ONE record parameter and that record carries
// `#[clientProps]`; `#[client]` cannot reach it, because `@Decl` does not
// expose a function's parameters.
//
// The whitelist is CLOSED: `string`, `i32`, `f64`, `bool`. A type not on it is
// refused rather than guessed at. `Element` is not on it — a client component
// receives server-rendered children as CHILDREN, never as a prop, which is the
// rule `serverSlot` below marks in the markup.
//
// **The whitelist is four names and not six, and that is a deliberate
// tightening of the front's spec.** § Step 2 asks for `string[]` and `i32[]`
// as well. `Field.typeName` cannot express either, measured against `2e6bb4ac`
// over a record with one field of each shape — the reflected names are:
//
//     a: string                   -> "string"
//     b: i32                      -> "i32"
//     c: f64                      -> "f64"
//     d: bool                     -> "bool"
//     e: Array<string>            -> "Array"
//     f: string[]                 -> ""
//     g: Array<i32>               -> "Array"
//     h: Element                  -> "Element"
//     i: fn(x: i32) -> i32        -> ""
//     j: #(string, string)        -> ""
//     k: Array<#(string, string)> -> "Array"
//
// The element type is ERASED, so `Array<string>` and `Array<Element>` are the
// same string. Admitting `"Array"` would admit an array of `Element`s and an
// array of function values through a check whose whole purpose is to refuse
// exactly those — a silent hole in the boundary. Under decision 67 the
// refusal wins: no array crosses today, an array-valued prop is spelled as an
// encoded `string` until reflection can name its element type, and the gap is
// reported rather than worked around (the front's first § Language gaps row
// widens from "no parameters on `Decl`" to "no ELEMENT TYPE on `Field`").
//
// The `""` rows are why a function-typed prop and a tuple-typed prop are
// refused with a message that says reflection could not name the type instead
// of quoting an empty string: they are refused because they are not on the
// whitelist, not because they were identified.
pub fn clientProps(comptime decl: @Decl) {
    if (decl.kind != DeclKind.Type) decl.fail("#[clientProps] must annotate a type");
    if (decl.variants.length > 0) decl.fail("#[clientProps] must annotate a record, not an enum");
    decl.fields.forEach({ f ->
        val ok = f.typeName == "string" || f.typeName == "i32" || f.typeName == "f64" || f.typeName == "bool";
        val shown = if (f.typeName == "") "a type reflection cannot name (a function, tuple or T[] type)" else f.typeName;
        if (!ok) decl.fail("a client prop must be serializable; '" + f.name + "' is " + shown);
    });
}

// ── step 3: the island, and the ONE place its marker is spelled ─────────────
//
// `contracts.md § 2` pins the marker: `<div data-onze-i="i0">`. The component
// name and the encoded props do NOT live on the element — they live in the
// payload's `i` array as `[id, component, props]`, which is how the client
// finds the island without parsing attributes off the DOM, and what makes the
// boundary auditable: every crossing value is in one place, in render order,
// and front 68 walks it.
//
// **Decision 77: one definition, passed in, never two that must agree.** The
// string `"data-onze-i"` occurs ONCE in this tree, in `islandAttrOf` below.
// Everything else routes through it:
//
//   | caller  | calls                        | for |
//   |---------|------------------------------|-----|
//   | this file | `clientMount`              | the placeholder's `attrs` |
//   | front 23  | `RenderHooks.islandAttr`   | the pair for the ordinal it just assigned |
//   | front 68  | the same function, imported | the selector its entry walks |
//
// Front 23 assigns the ordinals (`i0`, `i1`, … in render order) and fills its
// `RenderHooks` field from here rather than spelling the pair a second time.
// **It must pass a LAMBDA** — `islandAttr: { n -> islandAttr(n) }`, never the
// bare name: a bare function name used as a value lowers to an unbound erlang
// variable, and a function-valued record field must be read into a local before
// it is called (`val f = hooks.islandAttr; f(0)`, never `hooks.islandAttr(0)`).
// Both are measured defects this tree records, not conventions.

// The id spelling for an ordinal. Front 23 owns WHICH ordinal; this owns what
// an ordinal is written as, so the markup and the payload's `i` row cannot
// disagree about the id of the same island.
pub fn islandId(ordinal: i32) -> string {
    return "i" + ordinal.toString();
}

// The one occurrence of the attribute name in this tree.
pub fn islandAttrOf(id: string) -> #(string, string) {
    return #("data-onze-i", id);
}

// Decision 77's export: the marker pair for an ordinal. `islandAttr(0)` is
// `#("data-onze-i", "i0")`.
pub fn islandAttr(ordinal: i32) -> #(string, string) {
    return islandAttrOf(islandId(ordinal));
}

// One island: the id front 23 assigned, the component front 68 must start, and
// the props that cross the boundary. `props` is a pair list and not a record,
// because this is the erased form every island shares once `#[clientProps]` has
// had its say about the record it came from.
pub type Island(
    id: string,
    component: string,
    props: Array<#(string, string)>,
)

// What the server emits for a client component. The element carries the id and
// NOTHING else; children render inside it, unmodified.
//
// It touches no host cell, so it renders identically during front 23's BEAM
// pass and during a client re-render — which is the point: a placeholder that
// did not render on the server would be a boundary that never starts.
pub fn clientMount(island: Island, children: Children) -> Element {
    return Element(
        tag: "div",
        value: "",
        children: children,
        attrs: [islandAttrOf(island.id)],
    );
}

// The payload `i` row for one island: `[id, component, props]`, the props
// encoded `k=v&k=v`.
//
// The encoder is front 26's `encodePairs`, not `std/querystring.stringify`: the
// front's § Mechanism asks for `querystring.stringify` and that module is dead
// on the erlang row (`repro/erlang-std-slice-shim/`), which is why front 26
// spelled the codec in `router.bp` in the first place. "One encoder for the
// whole milestone" is better served by reusing the one that exists than by
// adding a third. It does NOT percent-encode — a prop value containing `&` or
// `=` does not round-trip, and that is front 26's codec to widen, not a second
// answer to grow here.
//
// This front does not build the payload and does not escape it. Front 23
// collects one `islandEntry` per island into the `i` key and escapes the whole
// script per `contracts.md § 2`.
pub fn islandEntry(island: Island) -> #(string, string, string) {
    return #(island.id, island.component, encodePairs(island.props));
}

// The pure half of the front's `propsFor(name)`: the decode, over the raw
// `k=v&k=v` string the host cell would answer. `propsFor` is this plus the cell
// (see this file's header for why the cell is not declared).
//
// `propsOf("")` is `[]` — the front's "a component with no props returns `[]`"
// acceptance row, asserted without a host.
pub fn propsOf(raw: string) -> Array<#(string, string)> {
    return decodePairs(raw);
}

// ── step 3b: the hole in the payload ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A client component may wrap server-rendered children: the Context Provider
// pattern puts a `'use client'` provider in the root layout with the entire
// server tree inside it. The provider is client code; its children are not.
//
// So the payload has a HOLE: inside `data-onze-i`, the subtree is server markup
// the client must ADOPT as-is and must not re-render — re-rendering it would
// need the server's data and the server's secrets. jhonstart marks the hole
// explicitly rather than leaving it implicit.
//
// The three rules that follow, and that front 68 enforces — none of them here:
//
//   1. A `data-onze-s` subtree is adopted by the client reconciler, never
//      reconstructed.
//   2. A server component may be a CHILD of a client component. It may never be
//      a PROP of one — which is why `Element` is off the whitelist above.
//   3. A client component may not read request scope. `request()`, `cookies()`
//      and `headers()` are `server.bp`'s and reaching them from a `#[client]`
//      module is a BUILD failure, not a runtime one.
//
// Same decision-77 treatment as the island: `"data-onze-s"` occurs once.
pub fn serverSlotAttr() -> #(string, string) {
    return #("data-onze-s", "1");
}

pub fn serverSlot(children: Children) -> Element {
    return Element(
        tag: "div",
        value: "",
        children: children,
        attrs: [serverSlotAttr()],
    );
}

// ── step 4: `server-only`, the poison pill ──────────────────────────────────
//
// `import 'server-only'` upstream is a module that exists only to fail the
// build when it lands in the client graph. This is the same trick in botopink's
// vocabulary: a module that touches the server imports `serverOnly`, and front
// 68 fails the build when a module reachable from a `#[client]` component
// imports it.
//
// **The returned value is meaningless and is never read.** Its PRESENCE in a
// module's import list is the whole signal. Nothing in this tree checks for it
// today — the check is a module-graph walk and the graph is front 68's.
pub fn serverOnly() -> i32 {
    return 1;
}

// ── the front-68 contract ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Everything front 68 needs from this front, and where each row comes from.
// Mirrored in `../../../docs.md` § *The client boundary*; front 68's README
// cites it there.
//
//   | Front 68 input              | Produced by |
//   |-----------------------------|-------------|
//   | the set of client component names | the `__jhClient_<Name>` functions `#[client]` emits |
//   | the island rows             | `islandEntry` per island, collected into the payload's `i` key by front 23 |
//   | the island selector         | `islandAttr(ordinal)` — decision 77, the only spelling of the pair |
//   | the client module graph     | the transitive imports of every module declaring one |
//   | the poison-pill predicate   | a module in that graph importing `serverOnly` |
//   | the request-scope predicate | a module in that graph importing `request`/`cookies`/`headers` from `server.bp` |
//
// Front 68 must also supply what this file could not declare: the module
// `"jhonstart/client-runtime"`, holding `hydrate` (per-island) and
// `islandProps(name)`. Until it exists, `hydrate()` and `propsFor(name)` are
// absent rather than stubbed, and the boundary is a convention nobody walks.
